From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Open Video Tools contributors <noreply@ovt.dev>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/hevc: export motion vectors and encoding parameters

The hevc decoder ignores AV_CODEC_EXPORT_DATA_MVS and
AV_CODEC_EXPORT_DATA_VIDEO_ENC_PARAMS, which h264, vp9 and the mpegvideo
decoders honour. Implement both for software decoding.

With "mvs", every output frame carries AV_FRAME_DATA_MOTION_VECTORS: one
AVMotionVector per reference list and per unit of the motion vector field
(min_pu_size, the granularity of tab_mvf), in quarter samples
(motion_scale 4), source -1 for list 0 and +1 for list 1, as the other
decoders do. Intra units have none.

With "venc_params", every output frame carries
AV_FRAME_DATA_VIDEO_ENC_PARAMS of the new type AV_VIDEO_ENC_PARAMS_HEVC:
the PPS initial QP and chroma offsets, and one block per coding unit whose
delta_qp gives the CU's QP_Y.

The side data is computed when the picture is decoded, from tab_mvf,
qp_y_tab and tab_ct_depth. A picture is therefore not output before its
own decoding has finished, and with frame threads the output waits for the
picture's decoding progress. Undecoded areas read as intra and as CTB-sized
CUs. Nothing changes when neither flag is set; decoded pixels are
unchanged.

New FATE tests: fate-hevc-encparams (tools/venc_data_dump) and
fate-hevc-mvs (codecview), each with a frame-threads variant that must
match the single-threaded reference.

Upstream-Status: Pending [to be submitted to ffmpeg-devel by OVT WI-4.107]
SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 The Open Video Tools contributors
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later
Rationale: Open Video Tools uses the patched hevc decoder as an optional,
  fast H.265 motion vector and QP source next to libde265 (OVT spec
  chapter 07 section 7.8, WI-4.107; Appendix D.1). It applies on top of
  0001 (block-params side data) in the OVT series; upstream it stands
  alone apart from the version numbers.
